{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"edits\":[{\"path\":\"history/0/text\",\"value_json\":\"爸 is absent from Shuowen. A Guangya entry groups 爸 with 爹 and 㸙 under the gloss “father”; this records use of the word, not when the graph was formed. The selected redraws show 父 and 巴 separately, not earlier forms or a sequence for 爸.\"},{\"path\":\"history/0/evidence_ids\",\"value_json\":\"[\\\"ref009\\\",\\\"ref010\\\",\\\"ref011\\\",\\\"ref022\\\",\\\"ref026\\\"]\"},{\"path\":\"history/1/text\",\"value_json\":\"One historical dictionary preserves an account in which 父 was added to a father word written 八八 or 巴巴. A later study instead argues that the father word is phonologically from 父. These competing explanations do not establish how or when the graph was formed.\"},{\"path\":\"history/1/evidence_ids\",\"value_json\":\"[\\\"ref015\\\",\\\"ref042\\\",\\\"ref045\\\"]\"}]}"}}
